lenOS 1.0 — attribution and upstream provenance
===============================================

lenOS is a LineageOS 23.2 distribution for the Nothing Phone (3a) / (3a) Pro
(codename "asteroids", SM7635) with hardening and anti-correlation features
integrated at build time.

Component provenance:

- Device, kernel, vendor, glyph and hardware trees:
  NullDebris (https://github.com/NullDebris), BSD/Apache-2.0 per upstream
  SPDX headers. lenOS does not modify their licensing.

- LineageOS 23.2 platform sources: The LineageOS Project
  (https://lineageos.org), Apache-2.0. lenOS is a derivative work; it keeps
  LineageOS licensing intact.

- The hardening patch set in patches/ (OTHER_SENSORS permission, duress
  PIN/password with eSIM wipe isolation, automatic BFU reboot in init,
  per-connection Wi-Fi MAC randomization with DHCP-state unlinkability, PIN
  scramble default, and their Settings UI) is adapted from GrapheneOS
  Android 16 QPR2 platform sources (https://grapheneos.org, MIT/Apache-2.0).
  Exact upstream commit hashes are recorded inside each patch. These patches
  were previously distributed as lenOS 2.8.1 hardening (MIT) and are carried
  forward here under the same license.

- LineageOS provides the per-app network firewall (POLICY_REJECT_ALL) and
  PIN scrambling; lenOS only changes defaults and consumes them unchanged.

- Cromite (https://github.com/uazo/cromite) ships as a presigned prebuilt
  APK, GPL-3.0-or-later. Source: the pinned release tag recorded in
  config/VERSIONS.sh. You may replace or remove it before building.

- ReSukiSU manager APK (https://github.com/ReSukiSU/ReSukiSU, GPL-3.0)
  ships presigned when root mode is enabled; source for the exact build is
  recorded in config/VERSIONS.sh. The kernelsu LKM is injected into
  init_boot at package time via ReSukiSU's ksud boot-patch tooling.

- avbroot (https://github.com/chenxiaolong/avbroot, GPL-3.0) is a host-side
  build tool used for OTA signing and relocking with user-owned AVB keys;
  it is not distributed inside lenOS images.

- OEMUnlockOnBoot (https://github.com/chenxiaolong/OEMUnlockOnBoot,
  Apache-2.0) is an optional Magisk-style module; it is fetched pinned and
  installed by the user, never preinstalled.
